On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:14:57 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I'd like to have another tool that uses daemon(8) to daemonize it. It
> should loop and do the work, and sleep if there are no inbox messages.

This is posted as "[PATCH] src/lab-process-mail: Add a loop to run
under daemon(8)" [1]. It adds lab-process-mail-loop, which runs
lab-process-mail, sleeps LAB_PROCESS_MAIL_INTERVAL seconds (300 by
default), and runs it again. Each run already fetches and drains the
inbox, so the sleep only happens once the inbox is empty. The
environment passes through, so LAB_BASE_BRANCH and NOTIFY_URL work as
they do today. Start it with:

    daemon -f -o ~/lab/process-mail.log -p ~/lab/process-mail.pid \
        lab-process-mail-loop

and stop it with kill $(cat ~/lab/process-mail.pid).

A failed run stops the loop. A stuck message is still the oldest in
the inbox, so a retry would spend an agent pass on it every five
minutes; lab-process-mail has already sent the "stuck" notice, and you
restart the loop once it is dealt with. The cost is that a transient
failure, such as a fetch during a network outage, also stops it, with
no notice since lab-process-mail only notifies about stuck messages.
If that bites, the fix I would propose is a distinct exit status for a
stuck message, so the loop can retry everything else.

The loop runs the installed lab-process-mail, so it does not fetch new
versions of the code by itself. After installing an update, restart
it.

> Would it be better to have a dedicated script for this, or a -d flag? I
> think a dedicated script makes more sense so it can easily call
> lab-process-mail. It also won't risk coupling the daemon behavior and
> the program logic. I really like the idea I got from s6 of writing
> simple tools that process stdio, and using a tool like s6 or daemon(1)
> to handle the daemonization, instead of forking in the process itself.

Agreed, and the patch takes that shape. lab-process-mail is unchanged
and stays a single drain. The loop never forks; daemon(8) owns
detaching, the log file, and the pidfile, and the same loop runs
under s6 as is. It ships in the existing lab-process-mail package, so
"just install" puts both in ~/local/bin.

Your "[RFC] processing the entire inbox at once" [2] would change what
one lab-process-mail run does, but not this loop, which only looks at
its exit status.

Status: the loop is posted and ready for review; it was tested with
kyua and under daemon(8) with a stubbed lab-process-mail. Nothing is
needed from you beyond review.

